<p>hmm</p>
<p>I'm a neutral vote on the verge of an upvote. The problems which I mentioned in chat, while mitigated, are still present. I feel that there is still some fluff that can be cut down-extensive descriptions of the -2 instances tracking &quot;prey,&quot; which doesn't really contribute anything because the creepy part is the stupid speaker cone in their mouths, pretty long descriptions of the psychological compulsion, etc. Even if you want to keep those details, you can definitely tighten up the description section by just cutting out extraneous stuff, like, &quot;it's not connected to a power source.&quot;</p>
<p>So then, we get to the end, where we get the backstory behind this thing. It's a decent backstory, but I still feel that in this article, it's underdeveloped. One of the challenges of interweaving narrative into an article is that a narrative needs characters. Your narrative is pretty plot-heavy, but the development of the characters goes only so far as &quot;pissed that his land got jacked,&quot; and Matherson is just&#8230;&quot;polite person with weird undertones.&quot;</p>
<p>I think this is boiling down to a matter of priorities in your writing. You are still spending a lot of time on your basic idea&#8230;essentially the <em>entirety</em> of your fairly long description center&#8230;but it's hard for me to upvote on that alone. That being the case, I have to read through all of your description to get to the morsels of a story that you've left at the bottom. You want your basic idea to be conveyed quickly and to the point, <em>then</em> you can put detail into your narrative.</p>

