														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2032463">
									

<p>I thought about what you were saying, and although I really do value your opinion, I'm not sure that I agree. The thing is, at their heart, these articles are still clinical reports. I imagine the Foundation would care less about the squabbles of two men who died decades ago, and more about the humanoid jukeboxes that can teleport and kill people. With that in mind, I don't see why they wouldn't want the bulk of the detail to come in the form of description and containment information. Now, I understand from an entertainment standpoint, the longer description might not be captivating, and it may feel like there's some being left out when it comes to the &quot;plot&quot;, but that's how it would be, realistically. They wouldn't have access to much of &quot;the story&quot;, but would have plenty of information about the thing itself.</p>
<p>I may be way off base here, but that was just my line of thinking.</p>

