    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2032653">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2032653">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2032653)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2032653">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1400175917 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 May 2014 17:45</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2032653">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;line-by-line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Observational Site 81-2 has been established thirty (30) km from SCP-2812-1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't a mistake, per se, but the site has generally gotten away from the &quot;number in parentheses&quot; thing, as it's not really necessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Fences were initially constructed around the business plaza where SCP-2812-1 is located, but have since fallen into disrepair. The door to the building housing SCP-2812-1 is locked.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The issues with extraneous stuff in this piece begin here. We don't really need to know about the fences, and of course the door is locked.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2812-1 is a 1930's style record player</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like there's a better way to describe this. This is a step above &quot;SCP-2812 is an old-timey record player&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The player is made up almost entirely of wood, brass, and glass, with other smaller fixtures included (although nothing unusual of a record player of the time).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unless that's important to the narrative somehow, I don't think this part is necessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The runtime of the track is 5:14, after which there is ten (10) second pause, and then the track repeats itself.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not really necessary to include this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2812-1 does not appear to be connected to any visible power source.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You would think that the Foundation could have at least established whether this thing is plugged in or not. &quot;Does not appear&quot; sounds silly in that context, it is or it isn't. Also, the &quot;plays even when it's not plugged in&quot; thing doesn't really seem necessary, and thus falls into &quot;overused trope&quot; territory.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The primary anomalous effect of SCP-2812-1 is a powerful physical and psychological compulsion apparent in all individuals within SCP-2812-1's range of effect.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I find that &quot;thing what make you do stuff&quot; fails to resonate on any level for me about 99% of the time. There's no qualities about the item itself that would naturally cause any sort of reaction and therefore put force into a narrative, it's just straight-up mind control. I guess mind control is unsettling on some level by itself, but the ramifications of that have been explored in far better detail, here and elsewhere.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subjects in the town of ███████ universally believe that they have to &quot;get out of town&quot; and not return.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Either everyone in the town literally says &quot;I must get out of town&quot;, which would be exceedingly silly, or there's a better way to say this in a clinical document. &quot;universally believe that they must relocate&quot; or something similar would work far better.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>usually ending in the individuals (and, if applicable, their families) moving away from ███████ completely, regardless of the state of their affairs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Wait, wouldn't the individual's family members be affected by the skip independently?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Once they have sufficiently &quot;gotten away&quot; from the town and SCP-2812-1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See comments above about the &quot;get out of town&quot; thing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When asked about their actions, most will claim that they &quot;just couldn't take it any longer&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And again.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Of the 13.4k residents</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That seems like an odd abbreviation choice. I personally think &quot;13,400&quot; would look better here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it is estimated that only 190 remain as of 20██</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm confused as to why <em>any</em> people would remain, given the skip's effects.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The compulsion to get away from SCP-2812-1 seemingly increases as individuals approach the &quot;epicenter&quot; of the effect.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You may want to adjust the first part of the description to account for this, because I started reading this as the skip affecting all people within its radius (which is what you actually write in that part) and then run into this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Parts of the town will begin to show signs of being &quot;run-down&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a recurring thing with this article. You can't just put prose in quotes and then drop it in a clinical document. The quotes don't suddenly make phrases take on specific meanings such as would be necessary to describe something from a scientific viewpoint.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The effects of SCP-2812-1 seem to be enhanced by SCP-2812-2, and at close range have resulted in severe cerebral hemorrhaging in ██% of those affected.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't get why the percentage needs to be redacted here. I know that's a common practice in older articles, but I don't see the actual reason behind it now.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, SCP-2812-2 instances are not immaterial; they can be driven off with light arms fire, and those struck will disappear and reappear at a later time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure why the default supposition is that the speaker monsters are ghosts.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Fortunately, due to the duration in which SCP-2812-1 has been left &quot;on&quot; (currently estimated at █ years), very few residents of the town remain as prey.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Fortunately&quot; is poor tone here. This is a clinical document describing an anomalous phenomenon. I'm also unsure why the term &quot;prey&quot; is used here; up to this point the monsters are just killing people for reasons. Do they eat the people or somehow gain some benefit from it? Because you haven't said anything to this point about it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Interview 2812 Perry-A</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This didn't feel natural at all. She seems pretty clear-headed for supposedly being a lung cancer patient that's needed to be hospitalized for weeks, let alone not receiving adequate medical care. She also seems fairly comfortable with the fact that a strange robot has just showed up to interview her. Also, and this may merely anecdotal evidence, I haven't met a ton of people in the real estate profession that causally drop the term &quot;motherfucker&quot; into conversation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and as soon as I get a hold of another copy of the title to the land that <strong>I know you had stolen from me</strong> I will</p>
</blockquote>
<p>He bolded text in a personal letter?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Foundation agents on a routine patrol of the near-epicenter region of SCP-2812-1's range of effect reported that the music playing from SCP-2812-1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I thought you said that people can't reliably go in to investigate this?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As a result of Event 2812-Epsilon, the effective range of SCP-2812-1 has been increased to ██ km,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Given the lack of understanding of the pseudo-scientific principles at work here, I have a hard time believing that the Foundation conclusively proved that the event itself caused the range to increase. The best it seems that could have been done was to note that the two events coincided.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>This one doesn't work for me. I find that there's systemic issues in the execution, for starters, which makes this kind of difficult to get through. There's this weird dichotomy of overly-detailed scientific description that instead of lending an air of authenticity merely lengthens the word count, and lapses into prose that are ineffectively handwaved by adding quotes around them.</p>
<p>I also feel like I'm supposed to find this skip spooky, which, hey, great, I love spooky. The problem is that the tools you're using aren't really up to the job, in my opinion. The record player feels like something out of a stock internet creepypasta, with the working even when not plugged in thing, and I just can't take the monsters you have seriously at all. The &quot;blasting old timey music at you with mouth speakers until you brain damage to death&quot; thing is just too goofy for me, and reminds me of Homer Simpson talking about dogs that shoot bees out of their mouth when they bark at you more than anything else.</p>
<p>I do think that &quot;death of the small town&quot; has some narrative force and could be adapted into a workable article. Unfortunately, I don't think this is that article at all.</p>

							</div>

								
